\begin{didyouknow}{Ten Split Into Two}
In his 1545 book \emph{Ars Magna}, Gerolamo Cardano asked for two numbers that add to 10 and multiply to 40. The answer, $5+\sqrt{-15}$ and $5-\sqrt{-15}$, struck him as subtle but useless. Yet his cubic formula sometimes passed through square roots of negative numbers even when the final roots were real. In 1572 Rafael Bombelli showed how to calculate with them consistently.
\end{didyouknow}

\begin{didyouknow}{Phasors Power the Grid}
Charles Proteus Steinmetz, an engineer later at General Electric, showed in 1893 that alternating-current circuits become simple algebra if each sinusoidal voltage and current is represented by a complex number. Derivatives turn into multiplication by $i\omega$. His phasor method spread rapidly, and engineers still use it to analyze AC power networks, though they write $j$ instead of $i$.
\end{didyouknow}

\begin{didyouknow}{Who Named $i$?}
The symbol $i$ for $\sqrt{-1}$ is due to Leonhard Euler, who used it in a memoir written in 1777 and published in 1794, after his death. Gauss later adopted it, which helped make it standard. Euler also introduced the letter $e$, popularized $\pi$, and found $e^{ix}=\cos x+i\sin x$. Every zeta zero in this book is written $\tfrac12+it_n$ in his notation.
\end{didyouknow}

