The distributive law $z(w+u)=zw+zu$ also holds. With $z=a+bi$, $w=c+di$, $u=e+fi$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:complex-add} gives $w+u=(c+e)+(d+f)i$, so by Eq.~\eqref{eq:complex-mult}
\begin{align*}
z(w+u)&=\bigl[a(c+e)-b(d+f)\bigr]+\bigl[a(d+f)+b(c+e)\bigr]i\\
&=\bigl[(ac-bd)+(ae-bf)\bigr]+\bigl[(ad+bc)+(af+be)\bigr]i ,
\end{align*}
which by Eqs.~\eqref{eq:complex-mult} and \eqref{eq:complex-add} is $zw+zu$. The associative law $(zw)u=z(wu)$ holds too: expanding both sides with Eq.~\eqref{eq:complex-mult} produces the same real products in each part. Problem~9 asks you to carry out this expansion in general and for a concrete case.

