Two equations in two unknowns usually have isolated solutions, so we expect the zeros of a complex function to be separate points that can be listed one by one, $\rho_1,\rho_2,\dots$, rather than lines or regions. This counting of equations is only a heuristic. The function $f(z)=\Re z$ assigns a complex number to each $z$, yet it vanishes on the whole imaginary axis: its imaginary part $v$ is $0$ everywhere, so one of the two equations imposes no condition at all.
\begin{beyond}
For \emph{analytic} functions, meaning those with a complex derivative $f'(z)=\lim_{h\to0}[f(z+h)-f(z)]/h$ as the complex number $h$ approaches $0$ from every direction in the plane, the zeros of a function that is not identically zero are isolated points. Polynomials, $e^z$, $1-2^{-s}$ and the zeta function (away from its pole at $s=1$, Chapter~\ref{ch:zeta}) are analytic; $\Re z$ is not. We take this theorem on trust; it is proved in the complex-variables chapters of~\cite{rhb}.
\end{beyond}

