\begin{beyond}
Two calculus theorems are used without proof (see~\cite{rhb}): a continuous function on a closed interval attains a minimum, and it is uniformly continuous there. Since $\abs{f(t)}$ is continuous and never zero on the closed interval $[t_a,t_b]$, it has a positive minimum $\mu$. By uniform continuity we can cut $[t_a,t_b]$ into finitely many pieces so short that on each piece $\abs{f(t)-f(t_j)}<\mu$, where $t_j$ is the left end of the piece. Then $\abs{f(t)/f(t_j)-1}<\mu/\abs{f(t_j)}\le1$, so $f(t)/f(t_j)$ lies in the disc of radius 1 about the point 1, which is inside the right half-plane. There $\operatorname{Arg}$ is continuous, so $\operatorname{Arg}f(t_j)+\operatorname{Arg}[f(t)/f(t_j)]$ is a continuous argument of $f$ on that piece (Eq.~\eqref{eq:complex-argprod}). At the end of each piece, the next piece's argument differs from it by a multiple of $2\pi$; subtracting that constant joins the pieces into one continuous argument.
\end{beyond}

